WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:03.560
Is Bane a dark reflection of who&nbsp;
Bruce could have become if he'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:07.480
followed a different path? Does Alfred&nbsp;
really see Bruce in Florence? We're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:11.280
here to shed some light on the finer&nbsp;
points of "The Dark Knight Rises."

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:15.800
Picking up eight years after "The Dark Knight,"&nbsp;
Christopher Nolan's third installment introduces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:22.440
us to a mostly peaceful Gotham City, a tormented&nbsp;
Commissioner Gordon, and a reclusive Bruce Wayne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:26.520
who stopped being Batman after framing&nbsp;
himself for Harvey Dent's murder. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:31.480
after cat burglar Selina Kyle steals Bruce's&nbsp;
mother's pearls and the mercenary Bane arrives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:34.960
and recruits the young men of Gotham&nbsp;
to his cause to tear down the city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:39.520
Bruce dons the cape and cowl once&nbsp;
more. Only this time, he fails.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.600
After Bane breaks his back, Bruce is taken&nbsp;
to a Middle Eastern prison to watch from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.840
afar as the former League of Shadows&nbsp;
member dismantles Gotham from within.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:52.000
Separating the city from the mainland,&nbsp;
Bane turns Gotham into a no man's land&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.400
ruled "by the people." Of course, that's&nbsp;
all a mirage, as Bane's men are parading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:00.360
a nuclear bomb throughout the city with&nbsp;
a mysterious triggerman at the ready.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:04.480
With Batman away, Detective John Blake —&nbsp;
a man who had previously deduced Batman's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:10.000
secret identity — steps up to help defend Gotham&nbsp;
in the Dark Knight's absence, but one cop and an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.760
underground resistance aren't enough. As Bruce&nbsp;
heals, he eventually succeeds in escaping from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:20.840
prison and returning to Gotham for a rematch with&nbsp;
his latest nemesis, even if it means his death.

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:24.800
By the time Bruce returns to Gotham,&nbsp;
the nuclear weapon has deteriorated to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:29.560
the point where it's set to detonate any&nbsp;
day. Alongside his allies, Gordon, Blake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:34.480
and Selina Kyle — who has since repented of her&nbsp;
own role in all of this — Batman takes to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:39.600
streets with an army of Gotham police officers&nbsp;
to take back the city from Bane and his men. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:45.800
Gotham police force overpowers Bane's men, while&nbsp;
Batman disarms Bane and demands the trigger.

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:48.760
"Where is it!? Where is the&nbsp;
trigger!? Where is it!?"

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:53.440
Only Bane doesn't have it. As it turns out,&nbsp;
Bruce's one-time lover and Wayne Enterprises&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:58.240
ally Miranda Tate is the real mastermind&nbsp;
behind Bane's plot. Unbeknownst to Batman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:02.960
Tate's real name is Talia al Ghul, making&nbsp;
her the daughter of Bruce's former mentor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:08.080
Ra's al Ghul. After Talia betrays Bruce,&nbsp;
Selina rescues the Caped Crusader and helps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:12.360
him track down the nuclear weapon. Batman&nbsp;
takes it upon himself to fly the warhead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:17.080
out into the Atlantic Ocean and allow it to&nbsp;
detonate far away from the people of Gotham.

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:22.600
As the city honors Batman, Lucius Fox, Alfred&nbsp;
Pennyworth, Jim Gordon, and John Blake honor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:27.680
Bruce's sacrifice. But as it turns out, Bruce&nbsp;
didn't die after all. After handing the keys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:34.360
to the Batcave over to Blake, Bruce and Selina&nbsp;
relocate to Italy, where Alfred watches from afar.

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:38.400
The last-minute reveal that Miranda Tate is&nbsp;
actually the daughter of Bruce's original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:41.720
mentor-turned-nemesis threw many&nbsp;
for a loop. Throughout the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:46.800
a story is told about a child born in a Middle&nbsp;
Eastern prison, the child of Ra's al Ghul, who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:52.360
climbs up and out of the pit. For a long time, the&nbsp;
movie leads one to believe that Bane is the child.

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:57.680
Longtime DC Comics fans likely saw this twist&nbsp;
coming: the most prominent child of Ra's al Ghul&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:03.400
in the Batman comics is Talia, who also happens&nbsp;
to be Bruce's on-again/off-again lover. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:06.240
aside from having led the League&nbsp;
of Assassins — an alternate name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:10.960
for the League of Shadows — Talia is also&nbsp;
the mother of Batman's son Damian Wayne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:14.720
the current Robin. Of course, in "The&nbsp;
Dark Knight Rises," Bruce and Talia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:19.080
only spend one night together, and their&nbsp;
passion doesn't result in a pregnancy.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:23.880
Though Talia isn't mentioned directly in "Batman&nbsp;
Begins," the first of Nolan's trilogy alludes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:28.120
Ra's al Ghul having a family of his own. No&nbsp;
wonder that Talia would eventually return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:33.240
to enact revenge on the Caped Crusader, who&nbsp;
she holds responsible for her father's death.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:38.600
From the moment Bruce is first robbed by Selina,&nbsp;
he becomes immediately infatuated with the elusive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:43.560
cat burglar. In a way, Selina is the one who&nbsp;
inspires Bruce to become Batman once again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:48.000
and she's by his side when he first confronts&nbsp;
Bane. Sure, she betrays him in the process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:52.280
but all sins are forgiven once she sides&nbsp;
with Batman post-revolution. No wonder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:55.880
these two end up together in the end,&nbsp;
each bringing out the best in the other.

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:58.200
"I like your girlfriend, Mr. Wayne."

00:03:58.200 --> 00:03:59.760
"He should be so lucky."

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:02.480
Though Bruce spends much of "The&nbsp;
Dark Knight" trilogy pining after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:06.200
his childhood friend Rachel Dawes, the&nbsp;
revelation that she'd moved on before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:09.800
her death opens the door for a budding&nbsp;
romance with Catwoman. More in line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:14.080
with the relationship between the two in the&nbsp;
original DC Comics, "The Dark Knight Rises"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:18.800
makes Catwoman a bit more three-dimensional&nbsp;
and complicated than previous incarnations.

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:22.280
While it may seem strange that Bruce&nbsp;
and Selina end up together, it's worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:26.160
noting that they both bring much-needed&nbsp;
excitement to each other's lives and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:30.840
while they come from vastly different backgrounds,&nbsp;
they quickly learn to better understand the other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:36.880
No wonder Matt Reeves played on this again in&nbsp;
Batman's next solo outing, 2022's "The Batman."

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:40.880
Christian Bale's Batman certainly has&nbsp;
an exciting rogues gallery. Scarecrow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:46.120
is a constant thorn in Gotham's side, Ra's al&nbsp;
Ghul nearly destroyed the Wayne family legacy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:50.840
and Two-Face sent the Caped Crusader underground.&nbsp;
Of course, few would disagree that the Joker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:56.040
was the most impressive — and most deranged —&nbsp;
criminal that the Dark Knight ever encountered.

00:04:56.040 --> 00:05:03.480
"You have nothing to threaten me with.&nbsp;
Nothing to do with all your strength."

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:08.000
However, Bane breaks our hero like no&nbsp;
other. Not only does he crush Bruce's body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:13.400
but also his soul. He would have broken Batman's&nbsp;
spirit too had our hero not risen above his pain.

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:18.480
In many ways, Tom Hardy's Bane is a dark mirror&nbsp;
of what Bale's Batman could have easily become.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:23.240
Likewise trained by Ra's al Ghul as a member of&nbsp;
the League of Shadows, Bane's desire is to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:28.200
Gotham burned to the ground so that it can be&nbsp;
restored, completely motivated by Talia's drive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:32.840
for vengeance. In "Batman Begins," Bruce felt a&nbsp;
similar pull and nearly killed the man who killed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:37.000
his own parents. He actually might have pulled&nbsp;
the trigger if someone else hadn't done it first.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:41.880
Instead, Bruce ends up taking the path of the&nbsp;
hero, hoping to inspire others to be the same.

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:46.480
Of course, Bane inspires a group of loyal&nbsp;
followers too. He even incites many of Gotham's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:51.000
citizens to turn on one another, exposing&nbsp;
the city's darkest secrets and challenging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:55.520
the status quo. If it seems like Batman and&nbsp;
Bane work from the same playbook, it's because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:00.600
they do. They each use what Ra's al Ghul taught&nbsp;
them, which makes Bane all the more dangerous.

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:03.840
"You're evil."

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:06.000
"I'm necessary evil."

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:13.480
One of the biggest debates among&nbsp;
fans is whether or not Alfred's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:17.800
encounter-from-afar with Bruce and Selina&nbsp;
at the very end of the film is real or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:22.320
in reference to director Christopher Nolan's&nbsp;
previous hit "Inception," only a vision of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:26.240
what might have been. The case for it being&nbsp;
nothing more than a daydream is a compelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:30.960
one at first glance. There's no way that Batman&nbsp;
could have survived that nuclear explosion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:35.360
right? When would he have jumped out of&nbsp;
the Bat? Did Alfred just imagine it all?

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:38.560
Of course, there are actual answers&nbsp;
to these questions. For starters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:42.560
the movie explains that Bruce fixed the&nbsp;
Bat's autopilot off-screen — which would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:46.400
have allowed him to bail long before the&nbsp;
explosion. There's also the fact that his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:50.400
mother's pearls are missing from Wayne Manor&nbsp;
— pearls which Selina had grown accustomed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:54.760
to. We also know that Bruce knows about&nbsp;
Alfred's yearly pilgrimage to Florence. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:59.600
it seems that Bruce wants his surrogate father to&nbsp;
know that he is okay, all without saying a word.

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:01.640
Lending his thoughts, Christian Bale believes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:05.680
Bruce's happy ending was genuine. He&nbsp;
explained to Entertainment Weekly:

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:10.360
"I find it very interesting and with most films,&nbsp;
I tend to say 'It's what the audience thinks it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:16.040
is.' My personal opinion? No, it was not a dream.&nbsp;
That was for real and he was just delighted that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:21.640
finally he had freed himself from the privilege,&nbsp;
but ultimately the burden of being Bruce Wayne."

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:24.840
Speaking of Alfred, it seems odd&nbsp;
to many that Bruce's surrogate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:27.680
father would leave just when he needs him most.

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:29.320
"Goodbye, Alfred."

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:40.400
Sure, Bruce tells him to, but we never imagine&nbsp;
he'll actually abandon Batman. Though all is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:44.800
forgiven by the end of the movie, it's still&nbsp;
strange that Alfred isn't involved in Batman's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:48.920
last stand. But when you consider how much&nbsp;
Alfred has had to tolerate over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.920 --> 00:07:52.720
and how much he cares about Bruce,&nbsp;
it may just make sense after all.

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:56.800
The question of what Bruce has to give to&nbsp;
be Batman hangs over the entire trilogy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.800 --> 00:08:00.040
but "Rises" finally addresses how&nbsp;
much Alfred is willing to give to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:04.360
help with Bruce's quest. Christian&nbsp;
Bale reflected to Empire in 2020:

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:09.160
"Alfred asks: 'How long is this gonna go on&nbsp;
for? It's not gonna be forever, is it?' That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.160 --> 00:08:13.520
to me has always been fascinating about Bruce&nbsp;
Wayne. And it's no secret — in 'The Dark Knight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:18.120
Rises' we come back to the original ideas of&nbsp;
what's happening in here, it circles back."

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:21.920
What Bale means is that Alfred has always&nbsp;
wanted what was best for Bruce — he just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:27.000
didn't believe that Batman is it. He always&nbsp;
supported Bruce's efforts to defend Gotham,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:31.200
but never at the expense of Bruce himself.&nbsp;
Turns out that going up against Bane is too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:34.960
much for Alfred to handle, though&nbsp;
he regrets leaving by the end.

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:39.760
At the very end of the movie, it's revealed that&nbsp;
John Blake's legal first name is actually Robin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:44.320
a clever surprise and nice nod to Batman's&nbsp;
longtime comic book partner. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:47.840
there have been a plethora of Robins&nbsp;
over the years, and in many ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:53.800
it seems as if Blake is a sort of amalgamation of&nbsp;
a few of them. An agile orphan like Dick Grayson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:58.480
an angry street kid like Jason Todd, and&nbsp;
a skilled detective who deduces Batman's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:03.480
secret identity like Tim Drake, Blake fills&nbsp;
the role of Batman's sidekick to a "T."

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:07.680
When the movie ends, Blake joins the longstanding&nbsp;
Robin tradition of graduating from the Dark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:12.320
Knight's sidekick to becoming the Dark Knight&nbsp;
himself. All three of the aforementioned Robins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:16.800
have become Batman at some point in the&nbsp;
comics, and others, such as Damian Wayne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:21.360
may do the same in the future. As "Rises"&nbsp;
ends, Blake locates the Batcave and is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:26.280
ushered into Bruce's old life, set to take his&nbsp;
mentor's place as the hero Gotham deserves.

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:29.800
While we won't ever get to ever see&nbsp;
Blake's version of Batman hit the streets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:33.960
director Christopher Nolan leaves his "Dark&nbsp;
Knight" saga open-ended on purpose to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:38.640
the legend of Batman alive. While it's unclear&nbsp;
if Blake will become the next Batman himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:43.720
or forge his own superhero identity,&nbsp;
Gotham is no doubt safer in his hands.

00:09:43.720 --> 00:09:47.440
The initial trailer for "The Dark Knight&nbsp;
Rises" splices a philosophical quip from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:50.560
Ra's al Ghul with clips from the&nbsp;
first two installments of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:54.640
trilogy — which ultimately sums up the&nbsp;
underlying message of the entire thing.

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:58.920
"If you make yourself more than just a&nbsp;
man, if you devote yourself to an ideal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:02.680
you become something else&nbsp;
entirely ... a legend, Mr. Wayne."

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:06.880
In "Batman Begins," Bruce constructs the&nbsp;
legend of Batman. He trains his body and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:11.040
his mind to become the crime fighter that&nbsp;
Gotham needs — even if he's not the hero they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:16.360
deserve — and saves his city by thwarting Ra's&nbsp;
al Ghul's plan and killing him in the process.

00:10:16.360 --> 00:10:20.160
Batman becomes a living myth who&nbsp;
inspires Gothamites everywhere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:23.280
and by "The Dark Knight," Batman&nbsp;
has fully established himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:27.320
even inspiring hockey-pad-wearing copycats&nbsp;
and theatrical criminals — like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:30.960
Joker — to come out of the woodwork&nbsp;
and make their own mark on Gotham.

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:35.760
"This town deserves a better class of&nbsp;
criminal, and I'm gonna give it to them."

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:39.320
By the time "Rises" ends, Gotham&nbsp;
finally recognizes what the Dark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:44.200
Knight has done for them, and honors him in&nbsp;
the form of a statue. But that's not all;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:47.920
Blake's decision to take on Bruce's mantle&nbsp;
— paired with the repairing of both the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:52.160
Bat-signal and the Bat's autopilot&nbsp;
— keeps the legend of Batman alive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:59.040
even after Bruce Wayne "dies." Ultimately,&nbsp;
Batman lives on, and that was always the point.

